    "body":  "## What\nA Sentry connector, so that someone who connects their Sentry account in Switch Console can let their agents look up Sentry issues, events and releases, as them.\n\n## Why\nAgents fixing bugs need the stack traces and context that Sentry holds.\n\n## How\nSentry has an official remote MCP server at `https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp` with OAuth and URL-only setup for any client ([docs](https://mcp.sentry.dev/)). The README also documents a token header, which the `api_key` shape could use. Self-hosted Sentry needs the local server and is out of scope.\n\nAdd a new entry, `core/switch_core/connections/catalog/sentry/`.\n\nFollow **[Adding a connector](https://github.com/sandbox-quantum/switch/blob/main/docs/design/service-connections-v2.md#adding-a-connector)** in the service connections v2 design doc. A connector is usually a catalog entry plus a skill, with no code, in one of three shapes:\n1. a remote MCP server with OAuth (example: `catalog/atlassian/`)\n2. a command-line tool with OAuth (example: `catalog/google-workspace/`)\n3. a key the person pastes in (`adapter: api_key`, with `mcp` or `cli`; example: `catalog/datadog/`)\n\nPrefer OAuth with dynamic registration where the vendor offers it, then a static OAuth app, then a key, and say in the PR why you chose that shape. If the vendor fits none of them, comment on this issue before writing broker code.\n\nLocally, turn connections on with `FEATURE_FLAGS_ENABLED=service_connections`.\n\n**Rules every connector follows:**\n- The token or key never reaches the coding tool (env, args or files); the session host makes the calls\n- Ask for the least access that works: no admin, org-wide or delete scopes without a written reason. Offer a read-only level if the vendor supports one\n- OAuth stays strict: HTTPS, PKCE S256, no new redirect hosts; don\u0027t loosen the loader\u0027s checks\n- CLIs run without a shell, from the vendor\u0027s official release pinned by SHA-256, with auth/config/admin commands denied\n- No real data in the repo: no tokens, tenant URLs or account ids in fixtures or screenshots\n- The logo is the official mark (viewBox and paths only), credited in `NOTICE.md`\n\n## Done when\n- [ ] `connections/catalog/sentry/connection.yaml` is complete and `enabled: true`, with comments on anything the vendor needs turned on and anything measured rather than documented (e.g. token lifetime)\n- [ ] `skill/SKILL.md` describes the tasks, tools and limits, with safe defaults (read before writing, confirm before deleting or closing), and never mentions credentials\n- [ ] It shows up in Switch Console\u0027s Connections with its name and logo, and connecting works (read-only and read-write, if both are offered)\n- [ ] Granted to an agent, its tools work in a session from a room; turning the grant off stops the session\u0027s access within a minute\n- [ ] An expiring token is renewed without the user doing anything (OAuth)\n- [ ] Disconnecting removes access (and revokes at the vendor where possible; for keys, the UI says Switch can\u0027t revoke them)\n- [ ] The token or key appears nowhere in the transcript, the room or the logs\n- [ ] Loader tests for the entry (`core/tests/switch_core/connections/test_loader.py`)\n- [ ] A short video of connect → grant → agent uses it in a room → grant off\n\n## Out of scope\nWebhooks from the vendor into rooms; tool classification by level; connecting from the web dashboard.",
